This is the sliding-window limiter. I'll keep a queue of the timestamps I've allowed, and since the input is already sorted, I can drop anything older than t minus window from the front. If the queue length is under limit, allow it and push; otherwise reject and don't push. That's O(n) time and space bounded by limit, not by the number of requests.

```javascript
function allowed_requests(timestamps, limit, window) {
  const allowed = [];
  const recent = []; // timestamps of allowed requests still inside the window
  let head = 0;      // index of the oldest live entry, avoids shifting

  for (const t of timestamps) {
    const cutoff = t - window;
    while (head < recent.length && recent[head] <= cutoff) {
      head++;
    }
    if (recent.length - head < limit) {
      allowed.push(true);
      recent.push(t);
    } else {
      allowed.push(false);
    }
  }
  return allowed;
}
```

The `head` pointer is what keeps memory down: instead of `shift()` on every request, I just advance an index, so the array only grows to at most `limit` live entries plus whatever's been retired. Worst case the backing array holds every allowed timestamp, so if I wanted strictly bounded memory I'd use a circular buffer of size `limit` and overwrite the oldest slot. Time is O(n), space is O(limit) for the live window.

